scenarios.lede:
Accounting prints its aged balance to a spool file.
- a header on each page;
- a client, its invoices below, a total;
- a client's invoices sometimes run onto the next page.
Credit control wants each invoice with its client.

A hospital pharmacy sends the hub its dispensing orders every day. The data protection officer asks for the AI to be switched off for the length of an audit.
- the operator sets LUMNIK_AI_ENABLED=false and restarts the hub: rag and llm stay installed, said INACTIVE;
- ask, search and chat answer the same sentence; the corpus is kept, and the hub says so;
- the files are still read; a lifecycle declared during the audit is not indexed, and the apply says so.

IT takes over an application inventory kept for years in a spreadsheet.
The URL column is typed by hand:
- mixed upper and lower case;
- stray spaces around it;
- a port sometimes there, sometimes not.
The manifest splits the URL into columns, without code.

An IBM i customer master, CLIENTS, CCSID 297: 30 customers.
- transferred in binary: no record delimiter, packed fields, every line dead-lettered with its cause;
- the IBM i's own export, CPYTOIMPF, read with encoding: IBM297;
- the next day: two customers over their limit, two decisions.

Dispatch takes over the delivery sites of a legacy ERP. Several tools typed the GPS points over the years:
- a decimal comma here, degrees-minutes-seconds there;
- a swapped pair, a 0;0 left by a geocoder;
- metres where degrees belong.
The manifest makes one point per site, or says why there is none.

Customers call: their order "never went through".
- the server writes a text log, one file a day;
- the ERP knows the orders, their customer, their status;
- no tool matches the two.
A business log, not a monitoring tool.

A business school's apprenticeship training centre (CFA), two CSV exports: 36 apprenticeship contracts, 40 OPCO invoice lines.
- an Entity joins the contract onto every invoice line;
- a Process compares two columns of the joined row: period_end after rupture_on;
- two ruptures reach the register late: the invoices already sent are judged again.

French banks still send statements in CFONB 120.
- 120 characters per record, no delimiter;
- 01 opens the statement, 04 is a movement, 05 completes it, 07 closes;
- the amount's last character holds its last digit and its sign.
Treasury wants each movement signed, with its complements.

An HPLC's acquisition software exports one report per injection: UTF-16, a header block, then the peaks.
- csv-file reads it line by line and carries the sample onto each peak;
- regex-extract cuts each value from its unit: 0,21% becomes 0.21 and %;
- an impurity above 0.15 %: one decision.

The sales team leaves a legacy CRM. Its contact export mixes:
- capitals and broken accents (BÃ©nÃ©dicte);
- two phone columns, one often empty;
- mistyped emails.
The manifest turns it into a usable list.

A plant receives its batches of raw material. Three systems say when: the LIMS scans the sample (an instant), the ERP books the receipt (a date), the carrier writes "Livré le" in a CSV (text).
- read as they are, the carrier's text makes the column text: twelve batches in dispute;
- typed, a date meets a time at the UTC day: the night shift's batch is in dispute;
- with zone: Europe/Paris, one dispute is left, the real typing mistake.

A sales engineer has dinner with Lumilait's managing director. They must answer without opening four tools:
- the packaging line: ERP and after-sales disagree;
- the retrofit parts: in stock or not;
- the catalogue: 360 parts to search.
Meanwhile, the CRM marks the deal ready to confirm.

A real cluster, five teams, five pods broken by Kubernetes itself:
- the pods API: state, restarts, why the last container died;
- metrics.k8s.io: what each pod uses now;
- the Warning events.
An on-call Kotlin rule sets the priority; the model translates the question, it decides nothing.

The catalogue of approved versions is a CSV. What really runs is in the cluster. lumnik reads each pod's image through the Kubernetes API and joins it to the catalogue:
- the approved version passes;
- a hotfix pushed with kubectl set image makes the gate return exit code 1: CI stops the pipeline;
- after the rollback, the same command passes again.

A valve manufacturer's quality department has tracked its customer complaints in a Lotus Notes database for twenty years. The Domino Data Service exposes it as JSON.
- start and count, the view's count in Content-Range;
- a multi-value field: a string with one name, a list with several;
- two critical complaints with no owner, two decisions.

A manufacturer and its logistics provider exchange flat files. No separator: each field has its place and its width.
- the header set aside and counted;
- eleven fields cut at their place;
- accents right, even in a one-line file.

The buyer sends an ORDERS message on a single line. Segments end with an apostrophe. An apostrophe in a label is written ?'.
- fourteen segments, in order;
- « Bâche d'hiver » whole;
- two order lines, each with its order and its EAN.

An ERP, a CRM, no shared id. Email matches the records. A shared mailbox points at two customers.
- matching by a written rule;
- two candidates: an ambiguity, not a merge;
- a human decides, and the decision holds on replay.

Yesterday, contact CC-42 and account AC-700 meant Nova Pumps. Today its email points at Helios Valves.
- yesterday's link does not move by itself;
- the contact becomes ambiguous;
- a human decides, and the history names the author.

Sales send an Excel report every month. One group per client, region and client in merged cells, a subtotal under each group. As CSV, a merged cell keeps its value on the first row only.
- the 15 subtotals set aside and counted;
- each row gets its group's region and client;
- nothing crosses from one file to the next.

An ERP exports its article catalogue every night. One label holds the separator, unquoted.
- the line set aside with its raw values;
- nothing removed from the catalogue while it is unreadable;
- the dead letter closed by the run that really read it again.

A Delphi stock application on Firebird 5: 40 articles, a WIN1252 database.
- jdbc-generic has no Firebird dialect: the apply refuses, and says what is installed;
- the export the shop already schedules, isql, is read by csv-file;
- a day of sales: three articles under their threshold, three reorder decisions.

Two chemistry analysers, HL7 v2.5.1 ORU^R01: segments ended by a lone carriage return.
- csv-file reads one segment per row, MSH, PID, OBR, OBX;
- the sample is carried down onto each result;
- a Process compares the value with the range sent beside it, whatever the flag.

An environmental laboratory screens micropollutants in water on an Orbitrap. The transients and spectra stay on the instrument; lumnik reads what the processing software exports.
- a key;value block, a header over two lines, n.d. and < LOD in the intensity column;
- every m/z kept with its five decimals;
- the air conditioning fails: the QC reads 3.84 ppm, the four samples after it raise a decision.

An export of supplier invoices carries two mistyped amounts and a test invoice.
- the three set aside, as they were read;
- the test invoice discarded;
- the other two fixed at the source, their letter closed by the right read.

An industrial group has three workshops, and each keeps its machines in its own desktop database, installed twenty or thirty years ago.
- Nord exports from Progress OpenEdge, Est from FoxPro for DOS, Sud from Access;
- each format asks for its gesture: ? for unknown, -1 for yes, a midnight time, a two-digit year;
- FoxPro's export, read without its code page, is guessed as windows-1252: the run says so and stops on the É of "Électroérosion";
- together, the three registers make 17 machines and one dispute: the grinder that moved from Est to Sud.

A metrology register and a LIMS, two CSV exports: 6 instruments, 144 results.
- an Entity joins the register onto every result;
- a Process compares two columns of the joined row: measured_at after valid_until;
- the register changes: the results already there are judged again.

A pharmaceutical wholesaler stores its medicines between 2 and 8 °C in two cold rooms. The data logger exports its readings once a day; an excursion is mailed to the quality lead, through the company's SMTP relay.
- on Monday, the alert of CF-2 leaves;
- overnight, IT changes the sending account's password, the hub keeps the old one;
- on Tuesday, the compressor of CF-1 stops: three mails given up, the on-call desk told by a webhook, the ERROR column says what to fix.

The marketplace export gives one row per order. The items are packed into one cell, and each reference packs model, colour and size.
- one row per item, even when two are identical;
- the reference cut into model, colour and size;
- a file whose column was renamed is refused.

An engineering office keeps its drawings in a PLM on Oracle: one row per revision, the PDF in a BLOB.
- the hub does not read a BLOB: the table is refused as it is, column named;
- the DBA adds a view without the drawing, its size in bytes instead;
- overnight, the archive migration copies two released revisions back without their PDF: two decisions.

A Progress OpenEdge ERP exports its Customer table with ABL's EXPORT: a .d file, 40 customers.
- spaces, quoted strings, ? for unknown, yes/no, ISO-8859-1;
- MM/DD/YY: a two-digit year must say its century, or validate refuses;
- the reactivation campaign: two customers with no order since 1999 and 2003, two decisions.

A goods receipt typed by hand: 500 lines, quantities like « 12,5 kg » or « 3 pce ». An operator's Kotlin script reads them. Line 317 says « env. 4 kg ».
- version 1: the run fails and writes nothing;
- version 2: the value declared unreadable;
- 499 lines written, line 317 a dead letter.

A carrier's tracking API pages by cursor. It reads it from the X-Next-Cursor header, and nowhere else:
- sent as a query parameter, the default, the cursor never arrives: the API returns the first page and says there are more; the run fails and says why;
- one line, cursor_binding: header, and the 1,200 events go through, 12 pages.

Drupal 11 exposes its content through JSON:API, a core module. The next page is in links.next, which Drupal writes as an object {"href": …}. lumnik has a preset for that standard:
- pagination: { kind: json-api }, and the 5 pages are followed;
- 45 articles read, 45 written to the hub.

A real FastAPI service on fastapi-pagination 0.16.1 and 130 fictional deliveries.
- quiet: all 130 in three pages;
- 500 per page: the library refuses, nothing is ingested;
- a size written in the path: refused at lm apply (#1789).

An order API can answer « what changed since this date ». lumnik reads it in full once, then each night only what moved.
- the watermark pulled back by a margin;
- an order changed during a run arrives with the next one;
- each date stored as a date.

Spring PetClinic from before Spring Boot, behind the server's login (j_security_check). No API token: a form and a session cookie.
- without auth, Tomcat answers its login page with 200: the hub refuses it, it is not JSON;
- auth: { kind: session }: the hub logs in and keeps the session, 6 vets;
- the app restarts, the session is lost: the hub logs in again on its own, once.

Spring PetClinic as it was before Spring Boot: a WAR on Tomcat. Its controller answers the whole list at once, no pages.
- /vets.xml: the hub reads JSON, it refuses the XML by name instead of reading 0 rows;
- lm source rest probe on /vets.json finds the list and drafts kind: none;
- pagination: { kind: none }: 6 vets in one request.

Support tracks its customer tickets in Gitea. Its API pages like GitHub's: the next page is in the Link header. lumnik follows the header, page after page:
- reading state=open leaves the tickets closed since open in the hub: they leave the API's filter;
- reading every state, by what changed since the last run, keeps the hub true: 57 open, as Gitea says.

A real Micronaut 4.7 and 130 fictional parts, served as the repository's Page.
- fifty per page: all 130 in three requests, the total checked;
- a part deleted mid-read: the total moves, the list is read again, nothing lost.

A NestJS service on nestjs-paginate and 130 tickets, 97 of them open.
- all: the 130, following the next link;
- open only: the filter follows every link, 97 read;
- a ticket deleted mid-read: the total moves, the list is read again.

NetBox is built on Django REST Framework: count, next and results in every answer, and a token presented by the word Token, not Bearer. lumnik names both:
- pagination: { kind: drf-limit-offset }: 120 devices in 3 requests;
- auth: { kind: bearer, scheme: Token }: the word NetBox expects;
- the next night, the 6 switches taken offline are in the hub.

A real Paperless-ngx 2.13 and 100 correspondents, read ten per page.
- quiet: all 100, each page's count checked;
- a correspondent deleted mid-read: the pages shift, the list is read again;
- new correspondents all the time: the run refuses to call itself complete.

A partner publishes 2,400 rows and their count. One night, a page of 100 vanishes, the count stays right.
- read and declared compared on each run;
- the faulty night ends Failed, with both numbers;
- the next night passes again, with no gesture on the table.

A real PrestaShop 8.2.8 and its demo catalogue. Its Webservice answers XML.
- not told, the hub refuses the XML and says what to add;
- response_format: xml: the hub reads the XML; PrestaShop ignores offset=, and the repeated page is refused;
- pagination: { kind: prestashop }: the 19 products, their name read from the XML.

An Express and Sequelize service, 130 orders and their lines, read by offset and limit.
- quiet: all 130, each page's count checked;
- with the lines, no distinct: the count says 260, the run refuses;
- with distinct: true: 130 of 130;
- an order deleted mid-read: the list is read again.

An earlier provider left a stock application in Spring Data REST, behind nginx. lumnik reads it with the spring-hateoas preset:
- its links name its internal host: the run refuses to follow them, and says so;
- one property on the application side, and the 63 pages go through;
- each product is keyed by its SKU, read from its link: the next night, no second copy.

A Spring controller returns the repository's Page. lumnik reads it with a preset:
- pagination: { kind: spring-page }: 130 articles in 3 requests;
- the team follows Spring's warning and switches to VIA_DTO: the total moves under page, the same manifest reads everything, one empty page too many;
- one line, spring-paged-model: 3 requests again.

A Spring Boot controller returning the repository's Slice, and 130 articles.
- quiet: all 130, stopped on $.last;
- 5000 per page asked: Spring serves 2000, the read stays complete;
- an article deleted mid-read: the Slice has no total, one article is lost without a signal;
- the same deletion under the Page: the total moves, the list is read again.

Stripe pages by cursor: each page says has_more, the next is asked for with starting_after, the last customer's id. lumnik has a preset of the same name:
- pagination: { kind: stripe }: 25 customers in 3 requests;
- the next day, 5 new customers: 30 rows, one per customer.

WordPress's REST API pages by the Link header and publishes its count in X-WP-Total. lumnik follows the one and checks the other:
- 46 articles announced, 46 read: the run passes;
- a "members only" plugin hides 15 articles, the count stays 46: the run fails and gives both numbers;
- read as a member, with an application password, the 46 articles are back.

Some orders arrive through a recent SaaS. It gives a client id and a secret, and wants a short-lived token.
- a token renewed before it expires;
- a key rotation absorbed mid-run;
- a revoked secret named, never shown.

A customer's SAP writes its orders as ORDERS05 IDocs, in a flat file. The order sits at the top of the IDoc, the article further down.
- sixteen records cut at their position;
- each line with its order, quantity and unit;
- an IDoc with no order does not take the previous one's.

SAP's Sales Order API, API_SALES_ORDER_SRV, served the way a Gateway serves it: October's 130 orders.
- odata-v2: three $skiptoken pages, the __count total checked;
- from_odata_date: the 130 /Date()/ values become dates;
- 48,000 EUR in status A: one decision, then a dormancy alert.

A real SENAITE 2.6, five research groups, 120 culture plates read through its JSON API.
- body-link: the 120 analyses in five pages, the total checked;
- 2400 colonies on Fleming's plate: one decision, key analysisrequest-24;
- left unverified: the plate goes dormant, a second alert.

A carrier drops livraisons.csv on its SFTP every night, always under the same name.
- each night read, then archived under its name and time;
- a file not archived said on the run;
- a changed server key refused until confirmed.

Shipping is computed by a Kotlin script the hub's operator provides. In December it is free from 150.00. From 1 January, from 200.00. The operator replaces the script.
- two orders of 172.50: free shipping in December, paid in January;
- each run names the version of the rule by its fingerprint;
- each order names its run: lm leads from the order to the rule.

A 2000s CRM exposes its data through custom SOAP services. The password travels in the envelope.
- one service read in one call, another page by page;
- the CRM's Fault on the run, with no retry;
- the next night updated on the keys.

A pump manufacturer handles its customer returns in a Struts 1 intranet written in 2007: Tomcat, JSP, DisplayTag. No API; the screen has a CSV export link, and a scheduled job fetches it every night.
- no header, ISO-8859-1, a quote escaped by a backslash;
- amounts grouped by a no-break space: "2 310,00";
- the next day, the closed returns leave the table, seven returns waiting for a part raise a decision.

Purchasing's EDI platform requires names in ASCII, upper case, at most 35 characters, and one id per supplier. The ERP's export has accents, long company names, inactive suppliers, and ids that read N/A, 0 or nothing.
- names put in the EDI's form;
- N/A and 0 emptied and counted, a missing id derived from the code;
- the next day, the same ids.

A health insurer keeps its members in its contract system and in its portal. With one source order, one of them is always wrong.
- the address comes from the portal, the plan from the contract system;
- every disagreement stays listed;
- it leaves the list when the source settles it.

A distributor exposes its open orders through a WCF service: HTTPS under the company's CA, a UsernameToken inside the envelope.
- the CA and the password live in lm secret, never in the manifest;
- the password rotates: over HTTP/2 the Fault is lost, over HTTP/1.1 it names the cause;
- three orders confirmed past their promised date, three decisions.

A WinDev application exports its clients with HExportCSV. The file is reconstituted from PC SOFT's documentation.
- read as it is: six clients, but all text, dates 20190312, Actif 0 or 1;
- three transformers: the dates as dates, the credit as a number, Actif as a real boolean;
- a question in SQL: the active clients with no purchase since October.
